Running peer review in large university courses requires fair reviewer allocation, strict deadline enforcement, and consistent anonymity between authors and reviewers — tasks that manual processes struggle to sustain reliably at scale. At TUM, the absence of dedicated backend infrastructure forces instructors to handle these steps through disconnected tools, introducing inconsistency and significant overhead.

This thesis designs and implements the TUMPeer backend: a REST API and PostgreSQL database that automates the peer review lifecycle for TUM courses. The three central contributions are automatic and equitable reviewer allocation using a load-balanced algorithm, double-blind anonymity enforced at the API level throughout the entire workflow, and a background scheduler that drives all deadline-based status transitions without instructor intervention.

The backend is built on a four-layer Spring Boot architecture and applies a ±20 percentage-point outlier detection rule during grade calculation. It addresses all ten functional requirements defined for the project, verified through manual end-to-end testing of the peer review lifecycle by simulating user roles and scenarios. A unit test suite covering the service layer independently of the HTTP and persistence layers was contributed by a team member outside the scope of this thesis.
